before txns impl, the db:
 - has double booking problem: Both Alice & Bob read the same key first at the same time & update it at the same time violating business logic (booking a seat in a bus).
 - has write skew problem: Both Alice & Bob read same/overlapping keys at the same time & update different keys at the same time (at least one doctor needs to be on call, both Alice & Bob check each other's status at the same time & set themselves as away).
 - has no support for rollback: whilst in the middle of an acc txfer, the db may crash leaving balances partially updated.
 - has read isolation problem: whilst in the middle of an acc txfer, a reader may read a partial state of the db .
 - has read committed isolation level out of the box: everything is instantly committed to the disk & only this committed data is read.

double booking problem: two txns read same val, perform business logic & update the *same key*
write skew: two txns read same val, perform business logic & update *different keys*
snapshot isolation solves double booking problem: Bob will realize that Alice has updated the key before he could & abort the txn because they are trying to update the same key. in this case the write set of the txn has the same key & read set also has same key.
snapshot isolation does not solve write skew: because two different keys are getting updated & the db doesn't know what the business logic is. in this case the write set of the txn has different keys & read set has overlapping keys.
serializable isolation solves write skew: it checks for read set to see whether keys in this set have been modified before executing the txn. if yes, abort the txn.

**from A Critique of ANSI SQL Isolation Levels paper**

  *dirty read* 
    Transaction T1 modifies a data item.
    Another transaction T2 then reads that data item before T1
    performs a COMMIT or ROLLBACK. If T1 then performs a
    ROLLBACK, T2 has read a data item that was never
    committed and so never really existed.

  *dirty write*
    Transaction T1 modifies a data item.
    Another transaction T2 then further modifies that data
    item before T1 performs a COMMIT or ROLLBACK. 

  *non repeatable read*
    Transaction T1 reads a data item. Another transaction T2 then modifies or
    deletes that data item and commits. If T1 then attempts to
    reread the data item, it receives a modified value or
    discovers that the data item has been deleted

  *phantom read*
    Transaction T1 reads a set of data items
    satisfying some <search condition>. Transaction T2
    then creates data items that satisfy T1’s <search condi-
    tion> and commits. If T1 then repeats its read with the
    same <search condition>, it gets a set of data items
    different from the first read

  *lost update* <- double booking problem
    transaction T1 reads a data item and then T2 updates the
    data item (possibly based on a previous read), then T1
    (based on its earlier read value) updates the data item and
    commits. 

